# Conventions

Project rules. One decision per bullet; each must be checkable in review. When a rule changes,
replace it — contradictions are bugs.

## Layers

- `konsistTest` enforces the layer imports and the ViewModel, use case and repository shapes below; a violation fails `./gradlew :konsistTest:test`.
- Dependencies point inward: UI → ViewModel → use case → repository → DAO / HTTP client. Never the reverse.
- UI holds no business logic. Repositories hold no business rules and return domain types, never DB entities or wire DTOs.
- Business logic lives in use cases: one class, one job, a single `operator fun invoke`. Use cases never touch framework types (DAO, `HttpClient`, `NavController`).
- A read that is only query → map goes ViewModel → repository directly; a use case exists only when there is a rule to hold.
- `androidApp` is the Android entry point only (activity, application, manifest, release config); all app code lives in `composeApp`.
- Shared code lives in `commonMain`. Platform differences sit behind one `expect` declaration with `.android.kt` / `.ios.kt` actuals.

## Presentation

- One feature = one folder under `ui/` with `XFeature.kt` (composables) and `XViewModel.kt`.
- Every screen has a contract `XContract : UnidirectionalViewModel<State, Intent, Event>` declaring `State`, `Intent` and `Event` inside it.
- `State` is a sealed hierarchy, one variant per real screen condition — no bag of nullable fields and booleans.
- Screens bind through `use(viewModel, navController)`, only ever call `dispatch(intent)`, and receive one-shot events through `CollectEvents`.
- Ephemeral input (a text field's draft) lives in the Feature and reaches the ViewModel only through an Intent.
- Non-error feedback is a typed Event owned by the contract; the Feature resolves its text. Use-case result types never reach the UI.
- Navigation arguments reach a ViewModel as an `@InjectedParam` constructor parameter (`koinViewModel { parametersOf(id) }`).
- `event` is backed by `Channel(Channel.BUFFERED).receiveAsFlow()` so events emitted while nothing collects are kept.
- The ViewModel never stores a navigation handle; it navigates through the `Navigator` passed into `intent(intent, navigator)`.
- Streams become state through `inViewModelScope(initial)` only.
- Each feature declares its own `@Serializable` routes (implementing `NavDestinationDefinition`) and a `NavGraphDefinition` registered with `@Single(binds = [NavGraphDefinition::class])`. All graphs go into the one app-level `NavHost` in `ui/main`.
- Features never import the app shell (`ui/main`, `ui/bottomnav`) or other features, except another feature's `*Route` types to navigate there; the shell composes features.
- A screen without state or logic may skip the ViewModel and contract.
{%- if include_bottom_nav %}
- Tabs are feature start routes listed in `TopLevelDestination`; the bar shows only on them and every other screen covers it. Switch tabs with `navigateToTopLevel` (back returns to the first tab, then exits).
{%- endif %}

## Concurrency

- Dispatchers come from the injected `DispatchersProvider`; never reference `Dispatchers.*` outside `DefaultDispatchersProvider`.
- ViewModels start work with `BaseViewModel.launch`, which runs in `viewModelScope` on the injected main dispatcher.
- Repositories are main-safe: a blocking call inside one switches to `dispatchersProvider.io`.

## Errors

- Every failure is a typed `AppError`. No strings, booleans or nulls as error signals.
- Library exceptions (SQL, HTTP, filesystem) are converted at the boundary that produced them and thrown as `AppException`.
- `BaseViewModel.launch` is the catch site for commands: it logs, converts with `toAppError()` and calls `onError`. A failing `Flow` that feeds state goes through `catchAsState { }` and becomes a failure `State` variant.
- User-facing text comes only from `ErrorLocalizer`. A failure *state* renders `ErrorView`; a failed *command* shows `SnackbarHostState.showError` (message + action) whose Details action opens `ErrorDialog`. Both keep the technical detail as a separate selectable block.
- `technicalDetail` holds structured facts (error type, status, code) — never stack traces, URLs, tokens or payloads. Full traces go to the logger.
- Never "Something went wrong": every message says what happened and, where possible, what the user can do.
- No empty catch and no catch-log-continue without an explicit decision about the failed operation. `CancellationException` is always rethrown.
{% if include_networking %}
## Networking

- Every HTTP call goes through `apiCall("<METHOD> <path template>") { httpClient.<method>("relative/path") }`; the label is a template ("GET /items/{id}") because it is shown to users — no ids, no query values.
- Paths are relative (no leading `/`) so they resolve against the BuildKonfig base URL.
- Wire types (`*Dto`, `@Serializable`) live in `data/remote` and are mapped to domain types before leaving the data layer.
- Only GET/HEAD/OPTIONS are retried automatically; a call that must retry a write decides that explicitly.
{% endif %}{% if include_desktop %}
## Desktop

- Every `expect` declaration needs a `desktopMain` actual alongside Android and iOS.
- `BuildInfo.isDebug()` is true only for the `run`/`desktopRun` tasks; packaged apps are release builds.
- App data lives in `BuildInfo.appDataDirectory()` (per-OS location, separate `.dev` directory for debug runs) — never in the working directory or `~`.
- Release packaging (`packageRelease*`) runs ProGuard: keep and `-dontwarn` rules go into `composeApp/proguard-desktop.pro`; anything loaded by reflection or ServiceLoader needs a keep rule.
- Distribute native packages (Dmg/Msi/Deb): the uber jar carries no version properties.
{% endif %}
{%- if include_library_module %}
## Library module

- `{{ library_module_name }}` is plain Kotlin Multiplatform: no Compose, no Koin. `composeApp` registers its types (`@Module` provider or `platformModule()`).
- It declares the same targets as `composeApp`; adding a target means adding it in both.

{% endif -%}
## Dependency injection

- Classes are registered with Koin annotations (`@Single`, `@Factory`, `@KoinViewModel`) in `commonMain`; only platform objects go into the `platformModule()` DSL, and annotated code that needs one marks the parameter `@Provided`.
- A new `@Module` class is added to `@KoinApplication(modules = …)` in `base/di/{{ app_name }}Koin.kt`; entry points start Koin with `startKoin<{{ app_name }}Koin>`.
- Dependencies arrive through constructors. No `KoinComponent`, no global or static mutable state, no `object` holding dependencies.

## Logging

- Log through `{{ app_name }}Logger`. `BaseViewModel` and `WithLogger` tag it once with the owning class; never pass a tag per call.
- Never log tokens, passwords, keys or personal data.

## Testing

- Tests live in `commonTest` by default (platform test source sets only when a test needs a platform API) and mock with Mokkery (interfaces only — a class that needs mocking gets an interface).
- ViewModel tests inject `TestDispatchersProvider` with a `StandardTestDispatcher`, collect state with `collectInBackground`, and verify navigation on a `Navigator` mock. Test doubles never live in `commonMain`.
- Each test names the behaviour it protects. No tests asserting that the language or a framework works.
- No test depends on wall-clock time, real network or real hardware.
- A test that reaches `BuildInfo` (directly or through the logger or HTTP client defaults) calls `initializeTestPlatform()` in `@BeforeTest`; on Android `androidApp` provides those values in `Application.attachBaseContext`.

## Comments

- Comments explain a non-obvious *why* only, never restate the code, and are never longer than the code they explain.
